I'm very new to the NetHack community, but I'm a hacker (the computer
geek kind, at least I try to be, anyway) and it makes perfect sense to
me to be anti-scumming and pro-spoiler.  Most hackers would sympathize
more with "Getting knowledge", i.e. knowledge from spoilers, than
"trying everything", i.e. branching out from a single savefile to see
what possibilities there are.

After all, hackers developed the Free Software Foundation and the Open
Source Movement, both of which are really just big "spoilers" on how
to program - if I can see how someone else implemented this or that
program, it's just a spoiler on how to do it.  No one should have to
re-discover everything by him/herself (hence the spoilers), and trying
all possibilities by, say, savefiles, feels much too similar to
shotgun debugging (i.e. trying out stuff just to make the bug go away,
instead of trying to, say, read it up and learn how to properly *fix*
the bug.)

NetHack is free software, and I'm reasonably sure that most people in
the community identify themselves with the computer hacking community
- I wouldn't be surprised if they have similar attitudes as the hacker
community.

